# Line endings
# ------------
# Every tracked text file is already stored as LF (verified with
# `git ls-files --eol`: no blob has i/crlf), so this rule adds no
# renormalization diff. It states that explicitly instead of leaving it to each
# developer's core.autocrlf, and eol=lf checks files out as LF on Windows too,
# which is what stops the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning on every commit.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Textual stylesheets are plain text; name them so the heuristic cannot guess.
*.tcss    text

# Binary formats. Git's heuristic already detects these, but naming them means
# a truncated or unusual file can never be mistaken for text and mangled.
*.png     binary
*.gz      binary
*.dat     binary
*.parquet binary

# Windows-only scripts are safest with CRLF.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
